</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.6 </span>Performance of <span id="A4.SS6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Germ</span> on Alternative Transformer-based Models</h4>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct our experiment to validate the effectiveness of the outlier removal approach using alternative transformer-based models, evaluating performance through Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) and average performance drop. We use the NT-500M-human<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/InstaDeepAI/nucleotide-transformer-500m-human-ref" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://huggingface.co/InstaDeepAI/nucleotide-transformer-500m-human-ref</a></span></span></span> as the target model for our evaluation. <a href="#A4.T19" title="In D.6 Performance of Germ on Alternative Transformer-based Models ‣ Appendix D Additional Numerical Experiments ‣ Supplementary Material ‣ Fast and Low-Cost Genomic Foundation Models via Outlier Removal" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> compares these metrics across NT-500M-human, <span id="A4.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Germ</span>, and <span id="A4.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Germ-T</span> models using different low-rank adaptation methods. <a href="#A4.T20" title="In D.6 Performance of Germ on Alternative Transformer-based Models ‣ Appendix D Additional Numerical Experiments ‣ Supplementary Material ‣ Fast and Low-Cost Genomic Foundation Models via Outlier Removal" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> examines the impact of various quantization techniques on the same models. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of outlier removal across diverse adaptation and quantization strategies, highlighting the balance between performance and resource efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T19" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 19</span>: </span><span id="A4.T19.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Low-Rank Adaptation Methods Comparison.<span id="A4.T19.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> This comparison evaluates the performance of different low-rank adaptation methods, including Full, LoRA, QLoRA, and LoftQ, on Nucleotide Transformer 500M models. The best results are highlighted in bold, while the second-best results are underlined.</span></span></figcaption>
